Grain growth at 900 °C was not further considered, as little change was observed after 300 s isothermal hold. Accordingly, non-isothermal results are presented for peak temperatures between 1000 and 1350 °C. Figure  4 a shows the measured PAG size for peak temperatures between 1000 and 1350 °C for three t 8/5  cooling rates (62, 38, and 27.2 °C/s). Figure  5  shows IPF maps of the reconstructed PAGs for the conditions mentioned above. Table  5  summarizes the corresponding average PAG sizes and the time spent above A C3  for all conditions is shown in Table  3 . Higher peak temperatures and slower cooling rates resulted in longer times above A C3  and larger average PAG sizes. Table  6  displays developed empirical relationships between PAG size and cooling rate for each of the tested peak temperatures. For all reheated conditions, PAG size increased with decreasing cooling rate. The most pronounced coarsening occurred at 1350 °C, while more modest increases were observed at 1200 °C and 1100 °C. At 1000 °C, grain growth remained limited across all cooling rates as seen in Table  5 . Fig. 4 Grain size vs cooling rate for peak temperatures between 1000 and 1350 °C:  a  PAG size and  b  effective grain size. Reheated conditions experienced a 1-s hold and 70 °C/s cooling rate and are plotted using open circles Fig. 5 IPF maps of reconstructed grains at 1000 °C, 1100 °C, 1200 °C, and 1350 °C for cooling rates of 62 °C/s ( a – d ), 38 °C/s ( e – h ), 27.2 °C/s ( i – l ), and As-Welded ( m ,  n ) Table 5 PAG and effective grain sizes as a function of peak temperature, cooling rate, and reheating condition Non-isothermal experiment
