Figure  4 b and Table  5  present the corresponding effective grain sizes. The effective grain size increased with decreasing cooling rate and increasing peak temperature, with the largest values observed at 1350 °C and in the as-welded condition. At 1000 °C, the effective grain size showed only minor changes across the cooling rate range. Table  7  displays developed empirical relationships between effective grain size and cooling rate for each of the tested peak temperatures. EBSD grain boundary maps in Fig.  6  and the HAGB density graph in Fig.  7  show a decrease in the density of HAGBs with decreasing cooling rate across all peak temperatures. Figure  6  also shows the peak temperatures and cooling rates that lead to the formation of the detrimental CM constituent. CM was observed at all cooling rates for the 1350 °C peak temperature. At 1200 °C, coarse martensite was present at cooling rates slower than 62 °C/s, while formation occurred at 1100 °C under slower cooling conditions. CM was completely absent at 1000 °C for all cooling rates. For the conditions in which CM was observed, the corresponding PAG sizes exceeded approximately 31 µm. Figure  8  presents SEM micrographs showing coarse martensite present in the slowest and fastest cooling rates for the 1350 °C peak temperature. In contrast, the 1000 °C conditions exhibit a refined microstructure at both cooling rates, with no evidence of coarse martensite. The micrographs further show that the Fe-10Ni weld metal microstructure is primarily martensitic, exhibiting the characteristic lath, packet, and block substructure.
